                          Scripts with --plan-id use that for both logic AND logging.
                          The flag is audit-only: it never alters script behavior, only routes
                          the executor's own log entry to the plan-specific log file.

Runtime side-effects:
    After every build-EXECUTING invocation (a notation under plan-marshall:build-pyproject /
    build-maven / build-gradle / build-npm, dispatched with the `run` subcommand), the
    dispatch boundary appends one kind=build entry to the unified change-ledger
    (.plan/work/change-ledger.jsonl, resolved via the tracked-config dir so plan-less
    orchestrator builds are covered too). Build-class-ness is the CONJUNCTION of both
    conditions: a query subcommand under a build-* skill (parse, discover, coverage-report,
    check-warnings, run-config-key, rewrite-log, find-project, resolve-test-scope, analyze)
    and a bare --help dispatch carrying no subcommand at all write NO row, because no build
    ran. Stamping a row for a pure query would let pre-commit-verify-freshness read that
    query's exit 0 as proof the current working tree was built.
    The entry stamps notation, plan_id (never null — a build invoked outside any plan
    is recorded under the NO_PLAN sentinel), args (the executor argv), command (the
    command the wrapper resolved that argv into and actually ran — a different layer
    from args), duration_seconds (the wrapper's measured build duration), outcome (the
    wrapper's whole stdout TOON as a dict), exit_code (recorded even when
    non-zero), status (the truthful build outcome —
    success/error/timeout/killed/unknown — derived from the returncode and the
    wrapper's stdout TOON, so a timed-out build stamps status=timeout despite its exit
    code 0, and an exit-0 dispatch whose payload carries no wrapper-claimable status
    stamps status=unknown rather than a green nothing substantiates), worktree_sha (the
    working-tree currency hash), log_file, and timestamp_iso. The stdout payload is
    parsed exactly once and feeds status, command, duration_seconds and outcome
    together; an absent or unparseable payload leaves those three None and the status
    derivation falls back to the exit code (nonzero -> error, zero -> unknown).
    The write is fire-and-forget — any failure is swallowed and never alters the dispatched
    script's exit code, stdout, or stderr.

Examples:
    python3 .plan/execute-script.py planning:manage-files:manage-files add --plan-id EXAMPLE-PLAN
    python3 .plan/execute-script.py plan-marshall:build-maven:maven run --targets verify
    python3 .plan/execute-script.py pm-plugin-development:tools-marketplace-inventory:scan-marketplace-inventory --audit-plan-id EXAMPLE-PLAN --include-descriptions
"""

